INT. 24-HOUR LAUNDROMAT - NIGHT

3 A.M. Fluorescent hum. Rows of dryers tumble sneakers and sheets. A TV bolted to the wall plays a silent infomercial.

ODALYS MENDEZ, 60s, small, upright, folds towels with ruthless precision. Her cardigan is buttoned wrong.

KAI MENDEZ-HART, 19, her grandson, slumps on a plastic chair, duffel bag at his feet. Hood up.

ODALYS
You could help.

KAI
I'm tired, Abuela.

ODALYS
You are nineteen. Tired is for people with mortgages.

KAI
I drove six hours.

ODALYS
I did not ask you to drive six hours.

KAI
You called me.

ODALYS
I called you at noon. To say hello.

KAI
You called at midnight. You said the dryer was making a noise.

ODALYS
It was making a noise.

KAI
Abuela, you don't have a dryer. You live in a retirement home.

ODALYS
The retirement home has a dryer. It makes a noise.

KAI
Then call maintenance.

ODALYS
Maintenance does not come at midnight.

KAI
Neither do I.

ODALYS
And yet.

Kai pulls his hood back. Rubs his face.

KAI
Okay. Okay. What's going on?

ODALYS
Nothing is going on.

KAI
You're folding other people's towels.

ODALYS
Someone left them. It is a waste.

KAI
Abuela.

ODALYS
I could not sleep.

KAI
Why couldn't you sleep?

ODALYS
Why do young people ask why? I could not sleep. That is a complete sentence.

KAI
Is it Mom?

Odalys folds a towel. Then another. She doesn't answer.

KAI (CONT'D)
Did she call?

ODALYS
She called.

KAI
And?

ODALYS
She says she is moving to Portland.

KAI
She's been saying that for four years.

ODALYS
This time she has a lease.

KAI
Oh.

ODALYS
Yes. Oh.

KAI
Did she say anything about me?

ODALYS
She said you are welcome.

KAI
Welcome to what?

ODALYS
To come. To Portland. To live there. She says there is a room.

KAI
There's always a room, Abuela. Then there isn't.

ODALYS
That is your mother.

KAI
That is my mother.

A dryer BUZZES. Neither moves.

ODALYS
Do you want to go?

KAI
To Portland?

ODALYS
To Portland.

KAI
I don't know. I have school. I have a job.

ODALYS
You have a job at a pizza place.

KAI
It's a good pizza place.

ODALYS
It is a very good pizza place. I have eaten the pizza.

KAI
Then you know.

ODALYS
I know that you are afraid.

KAI
I'm not afraid.

ODALYS
You are nineteen and your mother is leaving again. Of course you are afraid.

KAI
You're not.

ODALYS
I am eighty years old. I have no time left to be afraid. It is a luxury.

KAI
You're sixty-eight.

ODALYS
Eighty in my heart.

KAI
That's not how that works.

ODALYS
In my house it is exactly how it works.

KAI
Your house is a retirement home.

ODALYS
In my heart it is a house.

He laughs. Wet, surprised. She smiles, small, and doesn't look at him.

KAI
Why are we here, Abuela?

ODALYS
Because the machines are warm.

KAI
That's it?

ODALYS
That is it. It is three in the morning. Everywhere else is cold.

KAI
Okay.

ODALYS
Okay.

KAI
Can I help fold?

ODALYS
Finally.

KAI
Show me.

ODALYS
Thirds. Then thirds again. You do not crush them. You are making a promise.

KAI
A promise?

ODALYS
That someone will be warm tomorrow.

Kai picks up a towel. She hands him another. They fold in silence.

KAI
Like this?

ODALYS
Closer.

KAI
Like this?

ODALYS
Yes. Like that.

KAI
You're gonna be okay, right?

ODALYS
Mijo. I have always been okay. It is the rest of you I worry about.

The dryer BUZZES again. She nods at it.

ODALYS (CONT'D)
That one is done.

KAI
I'll get it.

He does.
